[2026-10-31T23:23:48.113Z] PALIMPSEST_TURN {"text":"All three steps are done, in order, without waiting on the research.\n\n**1. Delegation started**\n\n`palimpsest_delegate` (kind `RESEARCH`) returned `STARTED` / `RUNNING` against basis commit `ade62028…`:\n\n- ref: `dlg2.ade62028eca687a07466fe95a42e2f75993768cd.delegation-c30ff7d0b967904da9139e81977860b8.br-33370d938ff0499d187aa363`\n- state: RUNNING — the host job is live; no polling performed and none will be.\n\n**2. `deliverables/probe.md` written** with the two required sentences: what I started (naming the delegation ref and the research question) and that I am continuing with my own work.\n\n**3. Grant conditions listed** — I read `sources/brief.md` and appended to the same file:\n\n1. At least two of the three priority collections must be fully digitised and published by 30 June 2026.\n2. Any orphan works must have a completed rights risk assessment before publication.\n3. Programme spend must stay within £84,000, and the application requires a line-item budget.\n4. Nitrate film handling must follow the Partnership's conservation policy.\n\nFile contents now (10 lines): the header, the two-sentence probe, and the four numbered conditions with their source path.\n\nI did not block on or poll the research; its terminal result will arrive on its own and I can fold it in then — condition 4 in particular looks like it will bear directly on whatever the branch says about Northgate and the Filmtek.","toolCalls":["tool/call:palimpsest_delegate","tool/result","tool/call:bash","tool/result","tool/call:write","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/call:edit","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/result"]}
[2026-10-31T23:24:12.828Z] PALIMPSEST_TURN {"text":"The research came back on its own, and I've folded it in. I verified every figure it cited against the sources rather than taking the conclusion on trust — all of them check out.\n\n## What the branch found\n\n**Yes, Northgate can support Filmtek scanning of the nitrate collection, conditionally:**\n\n| Condition | Source basis |\n|---|---|\n| Scanner runs in the **inspection room** (16 °C, 45% RH — inside Filmtek's 15–20 °C / 40–50%) | `sites.md`, `vendors.md` |\n| Never the 4 °C cold store — Filmtek cannot operate there | `vendors.md`, `condition.md` |\n| Cold store exit via **pass-through hatch**, ≤ 40 reels, ≤ 30 min **including scanning time** | `sites.md` |\n| Files to CRO on **encrypted drives** (Northgate has no network) | `sites.md` |\n| **Two-week** booking, £1,200/day + £1,800 transport each way | `vendors.md` |\n\n## Two things I'd treat as the real finding\n\n1. **The throughput gap is load-bearing, not a footnote.** No source gives a Filmtek scan rate, so \"40 reels in 30 minutes\" is an assumption, not a plan. That is ≈ 45 s per reel — achievable only with fast continuous feed, and nitrate handling is fussy. If the real rate is 2 min/reel, the batch cap is ~15 reels and the hatch cycle count more than doubles. This is the single number to obtain before committing to the booking.\n\n2. **Rights gates publication, not scanning** (brief condition 2). Collection B is orphan works, so the per-reel assessment must be complete before *publication* — scanning can run in parallel with it. Useful, because it means the rights work isn't on the critical path for the scanner fortnight.\n\nMy own arithmetic, marked as mine in the file: 14 × £1,200 + £3,600 = **£20,400** (~24% of the £84,000), and 240 reels at ≤ 40/batch means **at least 6 hatch batches**.\n\n## File state\n\n`deliverables/probe.md` now has three parts: the original two-sentence probe (left verbatim as the point-in-time record), the four grant conditions, and a results section carrying the branch's ref and basis plus an explicit standing label — *cell-local exploratory, not Work Evidence, not Project Verification*. That distinction matters here: this is a well-sourced answer to a scoping question, but if the throughput assumption is going to justify a £20,400 spend, it needs a real measurement behind it rather than a reasoning branch.","toolCalls":["tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/call:read","tool/result","tool/result","tool/call:edit","tool/result","tool/call:read","tool/result"]}
[2026-10-31T23:24:12.829Z] PALIMPSEST_DELEGATION {"delivered":true,"text":"Delegated research completed.\nBasis: ade62028eca6\nExploratory conclusion: Yes, the Northgate site can support Filmtek scanning of the nitrate collection, but only under these conditions: the scanner must run in the inspection room (16°C, 45% RH — inside Filmtek's 15–20°C/40–50% range) rather than the 4°C cold store, nitrate must leave the cold store via the pass-through hatch in batches of no more than 40 reels for at most 30 minutes including scanning time, and digitised files must be carried to CRO on encrypted drives because Northgate has no network; a two-week Filmtek booking at £1,200/day plus £1,800 transport each way is also required. The sources (sources/sites.md, sources/vendors.md, sources/condition.md) contain no Filmtek scan-throughput figure, so whether 40 reels can actually be scanned within the 30-minute window is unverified, and the per-reel orphan-works rights assessment (sources/rights.md) is a precondition for publication, not for scanning.\nStanding: cell-local exploratory finding; not Work Evidence or Project Verification."}
